#{[17592186262862 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-2,1,-3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-2,7,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ & =⟨-2,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ & =14 \\\\ -2x+7y-z & =14 \\\\ -2x+7y-z-14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262887 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,0,-2⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨-2,0,7⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨-2,0,7⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ & =⟨3,0,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ & =-20 \\\\ -2x+7z & =-20 \\\\ -2x+7z+20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262888 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,1,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =-6 \\\\ x+y-z & =-6 \\\\ x+y-z+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262889 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨4,4,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+4y-2z & =0 \\\\ 2z & =4x+4y \\\\ z & =2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=0.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,0)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,-2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262890 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,5,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-z+17 & =0 \\\\ z & =5y+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=17.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,17)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,17⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,17⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,5,-1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262901 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,-2,3⟩,\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,-1,4⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,4⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & ={\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}\\times {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{array}} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =⟨3,-1,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z-10 & =0 \\\\ 4x-2y-6z+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262902 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,-3,5⟩$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨2,4,1⟩$  and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨1,3,-1⟩.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=⟨2,-3,5⟩+s⟨2,4,1⟩+t⟨1,3,-1⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$"] [17592186262903 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,-3,4⟩$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨1,5,-1⟩$  and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨1,2.5,1⟩.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=⟨2,-3,4⟩+s⟨1,5,-1⟩+t⟨1,2.5,1⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$"] [17592186262904 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $S$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$"] [17592186262915 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-3,4,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨5,1,-2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -2\\end{array}} \\\\  & =-9\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-23\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-9,-1,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-9,-1,-23⟩.$"] [17592186262916 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-2,8,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨3,-1,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =-8\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-22\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.$"] [17592186262927 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{UW},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨4,4,-4⟩-⟨7,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,6,-7⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{UW} & =⟨3,7,6⟩-⟨7,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,9,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{u}=⟨7,-2,3⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{u}+s\\overset{\\to }{UV}+t\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨7,-2,3⟩+s⟨-3,6,-7⟩+t⟨-4,9,3⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262928 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-4,2,6⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩-⟨5,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{u}=⟨5,3,1⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{u}+s\\mathbf{v}+t\\overset{\\to }{UV} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨5,3,1⟩+s⟨-4,2,6⟩+t⟨-3,-4,-4⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262929 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,1,-1⟩-⟨7,-3,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{p}=⟨7,-3,4⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{p}+s\\mathbf{v}+t\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨7,-3,4⟩+s⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩+t⟨-2,4,-5⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186275420 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram I is not a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case, we have that the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot \\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [17592186275441 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{-0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only"] [17592186275442 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only"] [17592186275443 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{-0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagrams I and III only"] [17592186287677 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies inside or on the rectangle's edges if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1\\le x\\le 5,\\hspace{2em}1\\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-1}}\\le x\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}5}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}}\\le y\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3}}\\}\n$$"] [17592186287718 "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=-x+5$ and *below* the line $y=x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<-x+5,\\hspace{2em}y<x+3.\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}-x+5,\\hspace{1em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}x+3\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287719 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-2$ and *below* the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-2,\\hspace{2em}y<\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-2<y<\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=-2$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287720 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-2$ and *below* the line $y=x.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-2,\\hspace{2em}y<x.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-2<y<x\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}x\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287721 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=-x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>2x,\\hspace{2em}y<-x+3.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x<y<-x+3.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}2x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}-x+3\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287722 "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=4$ and *below* the line $y=4x+1.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<4,\\hspace{2em}y<4x+1.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=4$ and $y=4x+1$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}4,\\hspace{2em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}4x+1\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}4,\\hspace{1em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}4x+1\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287723 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x-1$ and *below* the line $y=x+2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x-1,\\hspace{2em}y<x+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x-1<y<x+2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-x-1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}x+2\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287724 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=x+1$ and *below* the line $y=3x.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>x+1,\\hspace{2em}y<3x.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\nx+1<y<3x.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=3x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx+1<y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}3x\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}x+1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}3x\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287725 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=3x$ and *below* the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>3x,\\hspace{2em}y<\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n3x<y<\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=3x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n3x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y<\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}3x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}\\frac{x}{4}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287726 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-2$ and *below* the line $y=2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-2,\\hspace{2em}y<2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-2<y<2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=2$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n-2<y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}2\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287727 "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the curve is dashed), the inequality is strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287728 "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $x=-{y}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287729 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}<}\\hspace{0.222em}36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}9}}{x}^{2}+\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4}}{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}36\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287730 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the ellipse, say, $(0,5).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+4(5)^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}>}\\hspace{0.222em}64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}16}}{x}^{2}+\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4}}{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}64\\}.\n$$"] [17592186287760 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx\\in \\{-1,0,1,2\\},\\hspace{1em}y\\in \\{-2,-1,0,1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n-1\\le x\\le 2,\\hspace{1em}-2\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-1}}\\le x\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-2}}\\le y\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3}}\\}\n$$"] [17592186287765 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1<x<5,\\hspace{2em}2<y<5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to *include* the bottom edge ($y=2$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbol to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1<x<5,\\hspace{2em}2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y<5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}5,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}5\\}\n$$"] [17592186287766 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3<x<6,\\hspace{2em}-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to *include* the left edge ($x=3$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbol to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x<6,\\hspace{2em}-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\