 distributive property:\n\n$$\n(6\\mathbf{a}-5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})=(6\\mathbf{a})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})-(5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6\\mathbf{a})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})-(5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c}) & =(6\\cdot 2)(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-(5\\cdot 2)(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) \\\\  & =12(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-10(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-10(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =12(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})-10(-4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =24\\mathbf{i}-12\\mathbf{j}+40\\mathbf{i}-30\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =64\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-42\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295130 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b})=\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}\\times (2\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}\\times (2\\mathbf{b})=\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}+2(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}+2(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j})+2(4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+8\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =11\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295133 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b})=\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a})-\\mathbf{a}\\times (2\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a})-\\mathbf{a}\\times (2\\mathbf{b})=3(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a})-2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Then, we use the anticommutativity property and the fact that $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a})-2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =3(\\mathbf{0})-2(-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =2(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =2⟨6,0,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295134 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times (\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a})=\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a})=\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}-3(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\n- Then, we use the anticommutativity property and the fact that $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}-3(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}-3(\\mathbf{0}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a} & =-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{j}+-4\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295172 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n6\\mathbf{b}\\times (2\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b})=(6\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{a})-(6\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{a})-(6\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =(6\\cdot 2)(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})-6(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =12(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})-6(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})-6(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =12(-\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})-6(\\mathbf{0}) \\\\  & =-12(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =-12⟨2,-5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-24,60,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302288 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-3,2,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & ={\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}\\times {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7\\end{array}} \\\\  & =19\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨19,7,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =⟨-3,2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z+39 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302310 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨2,-1,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+2z+6 & =0 \\\\ z & =-x+\\frac{y}{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-3.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-3)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,-3⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,-3⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304808 "First, note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf{q}$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304818 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\mathbf{i}-\\sqrt{3}\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{n}=8\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{27}\\mathbf{j}$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot (8\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{27}\\mathbf{j}) & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\sqrt{3}\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (8\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{27}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot (8\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{27}\\mathbf{j}) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304823 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =⟨1,-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z+26 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304824 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,1,-5⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =⟨2,1,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =10 \\\\ 6x-2y & =10 \\\\ 6x-2y-10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304837 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨5,0,-3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨2,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =⟨5,0,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =13 \\\\ 2x+4y-z & =13 \\\\ 2x+4y-z-13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304903 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $D$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-6,3,2⟩$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨4,2,-1⟩$  and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨4,-3,2⟩.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=⟨-6,3,2⟩+s⟨4,2,-1⟩+t⟨4,-3,2⟩,\\hspace{1em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$"] [17592186304912 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,-5,1⟩,\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}=⟨4,-2,3⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,1,0⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & ={\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}\\times {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =-15\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-15,8,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z+67 & =0 \\\\ 15x-8y-3z-67 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304926 "The two lines have direction vectors $\\mathbf d_1=\\langle1,1,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf d_2=\\langle-2,-2,-2\\rangle=-2\\mathbf d_1$, so they are parallel. Their base points are $P(-1,5,7)$ and $Q(0,3,-19)$. The vector between them is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u=\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\langle1,-2,-26\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe plane determined by the two lines has direction span $\\operatorname{span}\\{\\mathbf d_1,\\mathbf u\\}$. These vectors are independent because $\\mathbf d_1\\times\\mathbf u=\\langle-24,27,-3\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf0$. A parallel plane through $C(1,4,-14)$ has the same direction span, hence its parametric equation is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle1,4,-14\\rangle+s\\langle1,1,1\\rangle+t\\langle1,-2,-26\\rangle,\\qquad s,t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nThis is the unique listed plane with that direction span passing through $C$."] [17592186304928 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,-5,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨3,-5,2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-1 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z & =-1 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304929 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-4,3,0⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨2,5,-8⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ & =⟨-4,3,0⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ & =7 \\\\ 2x+5y-8z & =7 \\\\ 2x+5y-8z-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308388 "First, notice that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=⟨3,2,2⟩,$ $\\mathbf{n}=⟨2,2,3⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf{q}$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ & =⟨3,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308389 "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨4,0,2⟩=16$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=⟨4,0,2⟩.$ So, $\\mathbf{v}⊥\\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4{t}_{1}+2{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{3} & =-4{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{3} & =-2{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}_{2},\\hspace{0.167em}-2{t}_{1}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{1},\\hspace{0.167em}0,\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{-2}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186308399 "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,-4,2⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,6,-4⟩-⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-1,5,-1⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308412 "First, notice that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=5\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{n}=6\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}$ is normal to the plane. \n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf{q}$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot (6\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) & =(5\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (6\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot (6\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311649 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,-2,5⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,-4,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨3,-4,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,-4,-2⟩ & =⟨1,-2,5⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,-2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,-4,-2⟩ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,-2⟩ & =1 \\\\ 3x-4y-2z & =1 \\\\ 3x-4y-2z-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311650 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-3,4,1⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨2,3,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨2,3,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =⟨-3,4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =5 \\\\ 2x+3y-z & =5 \\\\ 2x+3y-z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311651 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,-4,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,-4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ & =12 \\\\ x-4y+2z & =12 \\\\ x-4y+2z-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311664 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-2,3,1⟩.$  From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,-5,2⟩.$  So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨3,-5,2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =⟨-2,3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-19 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z & =-19 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311665 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,1,0⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨0,4,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,4,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4 \\\\ 4y+z & =4 \\\\ 4y+z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311666 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨3,1,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 2z & =-3x-y-4 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-2)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨3,1,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311667 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨5,-7,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-7y+4z+20 & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-5.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-5)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,-5⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,-5⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,-7,4⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311668 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨5,2,1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y+z-4 & =0 \\\\ z & =-5x-2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,4)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,4⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em},1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311669 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨5,0,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2z-8 & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0,$ we get $z=4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,4)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,4⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,0,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311670 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨9,-3,0⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y+6 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =-9x-6 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,2,0)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,2,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,2,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨9,-3,0⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311671 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,12,-6⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12y-6z-30 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-12y+30 \\\\ z & =2y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=-5.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-5)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,-5⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,-5⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,12,-6⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311692 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-4,3,5⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & ={\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}\\times {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8\\end{array}} \\\\  & =36\\mathbf{i}+16\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨36,16,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =⟨-4,3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z+76 & =0 \\\\ 9x+4y+z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311695 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,4,2⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨-1,2,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,3⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & ={\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}\\times {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =0\\mathbf{i}-8\\mathbf{j}+8\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,-8,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =⟨3,4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z+16 & =0 \\\\ y-z-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311696 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,-2,1⟩,\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}=⟨5,0,3⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,3⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & ={\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}\\times {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{array}} \\\\  & =-10\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+11\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-10,4,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =⟨5,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17 \\\\ -10x+4y+11z & =-17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z & =17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z-17 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311697 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,-3,1⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨4,0,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨4,0,-2⟩,\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ & =⟨2,-3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute  $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ & =3 \\\\ 4x-2z & =6 \\\\ 4x-2z-6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311708 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,-4,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & ={\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}\\times {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =11\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨11,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =⟨2,-4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z-30 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311717 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,-2,1⟩.$ From the Cartesian equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨5,0,-3⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨5,0,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ & =⟨3,-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ & =12 \\\\ 5x-3z & =12 \\\\ 5x-3z-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311718 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,-4,1⟩.$  From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,4,0⟩.$  So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,4,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ & =⟨2,-4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ & =-14 \\\\ x+4y & =-14 \\\\ x+4y+14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186311921 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-3,5,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨4,7,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =-33\\mathbf{i}+13\\mathbf{j}-41\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-33,13,-41⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-33,13,-41⟩.$"] [17592186311922 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨3,-9,-5⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨-8,9,3⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & -9 & -5 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 3\\end{array}} \\\\  & =18\\mathbf{i}+31\\mathbf{j}-45\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨18,31,-45⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨18,31,-45⟩.$"] [17592186311923 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-5,1,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨3,1,-2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -5 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{array}} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-7\\mathbf{j}-8\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-7,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-3,-7,-8⟩.$"] [17592186314753 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,-1,1⟩,\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,0,3⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & ={\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}\\times {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =-5\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-5,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 5x-y-2z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186314765 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =5 \\\\ 2x+z & =5 \\\\ 2x+z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317172 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot \\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot \\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only."] [17592186317349 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only"] [17592186323703 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace.  Every subspace must contain the origin,  but from the diagram, we can easily see that the origin is not in the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$  without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only."] [17592186338825 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,1,3⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =-6 \\\\ x+y+3z & =-6 \\\\ x+y+3z+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338826 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,3,-1⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,2,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨3,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =⟨2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =14 \\\\ 3x+2y-2z & =14 \\\\ 3x+2y-2z-14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
